7 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455367 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455367 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186455012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455367 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "63750d47-51f3-5cdf-a76b-72522571295c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186454971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186455311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455391 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455391 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455391 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186454915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455391 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "98645239-f570-5bdc-ae35-ee32a0ef62bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455435 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b9e5c87-171d-5626-ae59-fa7b69d9a95a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455435 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y+1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}-2e \\\\  & =2e({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f1c8267-786a-5d55-8970-d1a062c41c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186483476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1045 "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186483447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482802 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5612666d-368d-549d-8c98-b899d091fd25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482817 #edb/ref 1080 "32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482817 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482817 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04d839b3-7560-5512-ab94-65b25afb8af3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482828 #edb/ref 1080 "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bd0b7d8-497a-5308-9309-036b9effbae0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482841 #edb/ref 1080 "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759